**Read:** [Mineralogy — Dexter Perkins, open editions](../sources/Mineralogy-Perkins.lingua#r_3Y3EJ93EG4V7Q0TZBCRRXVNVKP), the full opaque/economic-mineral and relevant optical material; [Handbook of Mineralogy — Anthony, Bideaux, Bladh, and Nichols](../sources/Handbook-of-Mineralogy.lingua#r_10TEF3RSN7XFMKTGYGY4WR3P85), complete selected optical/composition entries and their references; [Electron-excited X-ray microanalysis by EDS at 50 — Newbury and Ritchie](../sources/NIST-Quantitative-EDS.lingua#r_7XJDCAH01NX65F6Q5YY3CBFTCF), the full relevant imaging/quantitative context.

An opaque grain in a transmitted-light section needs a different observational account from a transparent mineral. Reflected-light examination of a suitable polished surface can reveal reflectance, color, anisotropy, textures, intergrowths, and relationships, but preparation and illumination conditions affect the observations.

Sulfides, oxides, native elements, carbonaceous materials, and preparation artifacts can appear dark in transmitted light. A bright backscattered-electron region indicates an instrument-dependent contrast under the measurement conditions, not an automatic species or bulk-grade result. Combine structural and compositional evidence.

Fine intergrowths and alteration can control processing or environmental behavior even when their modal amount is small. A point analysis spanning two phases can yield a plausible intermediate composition that represents neither phase. Record interaction volume, spatial context, specimen surface, and uncertainty when choosing follow-up methods.

**Work:** reconstruct a published opaque-mineral identification from reflected-light description, texture, diffraction/spectral information, and chemistry. Compare two candidates consistent with part of the observations and identify a discriminating measurement. Explain which claims could be made from transmitted-light study alone and which remain unresolved.

Read the complete opaque/non-silicate entries and relevant analytical treatment. Practical reflected-light competence requires appropriate instrument guidance and known specimens; an external image comparison is useful interpretive preparation with a more limited demonstrated scope.

